          "confirmed_text": "Reflected on completed work patterns, subjective dimension scores (elegance, design coherence, cross-module arch, test strategy stale), and mechanical backlog (exports, duplication, smells). Strategy prioritizes false-positive cleanup, then dead exports, then subjective re-review as highest leverage."
        },
        "reflect": {
          "stage": "reflect",
          "report": "## Reflect Stage: Strategy Formation\n\n### Patterns from completed work (round 5)\n\nPrior round resolved 17 review issues: 10 fixed, ~4 false-positives, ~3 other resolutions. Key wins:\n- renderSyncLines ownership fix (moved to correct module)\n- countActions utility adoption in settings-report-builder\n- getVersion helper extraction in program.ts\n- Dead barrel export cleanup in sync/index.ts and vcs/index.ts\n- JSDoc/comment cleanup\n\n### What moved the score vs what didn't\n\nStrict score went from ~84 to 86.4 through a combination of mechanical fixes (dead exports, unused imports) and review issue resolution. The remaining gap to 95.0 is primarily driven by:\n1. **Subjective dimension scores** (75% weight) \u2014 7 dimensions are stale and 14 are below target. The biggest weighted drags are elegance dimensions (mid: 87%, high: 90%, low: 84%) and design coherence (88.5%).\n2. **Code quality strict score** (25.9%) \u2014 this is deceptive. The \"strict\" metric on code quality penalizes all open mechanical findings heavily, but the actual health is 92.9%. Each fix has ~0.1pt impact.\n3. **Duplication strict** (81.3%) \u2014 boilerplate blocks in output module are genuine.\n\n### Strategy assessment\n\n**Critical insight:** The single biggest lever is re-reviewing stale subjective dimensions. With 75% weighting, even modest score improvements (e.g., stale dimensions going from current scores to 95+) would close most of the 8.6pt gap. Mechanical fixes alone cannot close the gap \u2014 they only control 25% of the score.\n\n**Recommended execution order:**\n1. **Quick false-positive cleanup** \u2014 skip the 2 security false positives immediately. Removes noise and improves strict score.\n2. **Dead export batch** \u2014 the 22-item auto/exports cluster is mechanical and safe. Removes barrel re-exports not imported by any consumer. Must verify each against test imports per CLAUDE.md.\n3. **Boilerplate deduplication** \u2014 extract shared rendering helpers in the output module to address the ~10 duplication findings.\n4. **Stale subjective review** \u2014 re-review the 7 stale dimensions (AI generated debt, API coherence, abstraction fit, cross-module arch, design coherence, structure nav, test strategy). This is the highest-leverage action.\n5. **Selective smell cleanup** \u2014 address genuine smells that are quick wins (unused imports, async_no_await patterns worth fixing). Skip essential complexity in validators/normalizers.\n\n### Clusters to consolidate\n\nMany existing clusters overlap or are stale from prior rounds:\n- `output-structural-debt`, `output-dead-code`, `output-renderSyncLines-fix`, `dead-summary-cleanup` \u2014 all related to output module. These may already be partially resolved after renderSyncLines was moved.\n- `barrel-hygiene` and `auto/exports` overlap \u2014 both address dead exports.\n- `stale-findings` cluster should be verified with rescan.\n- `accepted-debt` cluster is intentionally deferred \u2014 keep as-is.\n\n### Recurring patterns to watch\n\n1. Dead exports keep appearing because new code bypasses barrels. Root cause: no lint rule enforcing barrel-only imports.\n2. Boilerplate duplication clusters around the output/report modules \u2014 these modules use similar rendering patterns.\n3. Review false-positive rate was high last round \u2014 the observe stage confirmed 0 open review issues remain.",
